            <ArticleTitle>Cerebral processing of auditory and noxious stimuli in severely brain injured patients: differences between VS and MCS.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>283-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We review cerebral processing of auditory and noxious stimuli in minimally conscious state (MCS) and vegetative state (VS) patients. In contrast with limited brain activation found in VS patients, MCS patients show activation similar to controls in response to auditory, emotional and noxious stimuli. Despite an apparent clinical similarity between MCS and VS patients, functional imaging data show striking differences in cortical segregation and integration between these two conditions. However, in the absence of a generally accepted neural correlate of consciousness as measured by functional neuroimaging, clinical assessment remains the gold standard for the evaluation and management of severely brain damaged patients.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
